<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Düsseldorf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Düsseldorf is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Count Adolf V of Berg granted it town rights in 1288 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a>, and it became the residence of the counts and dukes of Berg and later of Elector Johann Wilhelm of the Palatinate, who made it a baroque court city. It is now a major centre of commerce, fashion and art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dusseldorf.jpg" alt="Düsseldorf" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Eubel from Berlin · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rheinkniebr%C3%BCcke_at_night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Düsseldorf lies at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> basin, where the Düssel flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and most of the city is on the east bank. It lies in the middle of the Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> metropolitan region. Its name comes from the small river Düssel, with the suffix dorf, meaning village, so that the literal meaning is village on the Düssel. It reflects the original character of the settlement as a fishing and farming community on the riverbanks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 7th and 8th centuries the odd farming or fishing settlement stood where the Düssel meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and the city grew from such settlements. In 1186 Düsseldorf came under the rule of the County of Berg, a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1263 the Count of Berg granted ferry rights to its inhabitants, and it gained city rights in 1288 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> it grew into a regional political and cultural centre under the Dukes of Berg and later as the capital of the United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg. In the 17th and 18th centuries it became known for its court culture and early art academy. During the Napoleonic era it was briefly part of the Grand Duchy of Berg. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, including Düsseldorf, was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. The city lost its status as a capital but became the seat of a regional governor and it industrialised rapidly in the 19th century; its population passed 100,000 in 1882.</p><p>In the Second World War Düsseldorf was the location of a Nazi prison with forced labour subcamps and of five subcamps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp">Buchenwald concentration camp</a>, and the city's Rabbi, who had fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> in 1943. The city entered the British occupation zone on 21 June 1945, and in 1946 it became the capital of the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>. Its population peaked at just over 705,000 in 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Düsseldorf is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and, with 619,444 inhabitants in 2025, the second-largest city in the state after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. It is an international business and financial centre, known for its fashion and trade fairs, and it has the largest Japanese community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Kunstakademie Düsseldorf, its academy of arts, counts Joseph Beuys, Emanuel Leutze, August Macke and Gerhard Richter among its members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Düsseldorf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the text began, other tunes were sung with it for many years: one with a paraphrase attributed to Christoph Bernhard Verspoell, popular in the Diocese of Münster, and one from <b>Düsseldorf</b> (1836) with Heinrich Bone's translation, which Johann Baptist Singenberger took to Catholic communities in the United States.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1288 Count Adolf V helped defeat the archbishop at Worringen and granted <b>Düsseldorf</b> town rights.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a></li><li><blockquote>He combined it with Cleves and other lands and gave it to his brother-in-law Joachim Murat as the Grand Duchy of Berg, with <b>Düsseldorf</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a></li><li><blockquote>Wolfgang William converted to Catholicism in 1613 to strengthen his claim in the Jülich succession and won Jülich and Berg, ruling from <b>Düsseldorf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a></li><li><blockquote>On his father-in-law's death in 1511 and his own father's in 1521, he united the Duchy of Jülich, the Duchy of Cleves and the Duchy of Berg with the counties of Mark and Ravensberg, ruling from <b>Düsseldorf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg">United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a></li></ul></section>
